<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/2nd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jugurthine War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">112–105 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bellum-iugurthinum">The Jugurthine War</a> was fought from 112 to 105 BCE between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>, king of Numidia. It began after Jugurtha seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and the Italian traders there, and Roman efforts were long hampered by bribery and defeats. Quintus Caecilius Metellus and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> gained the upper hand, and Jugurtha was finally betrayed to Marius's quaestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> by King Bocchus of Mauretania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jugurthine-war.jpg" alt="Jugurthine War" width="320" height="144" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sulla_Coin2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Numidia was a kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, roughly corresponding to the north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, next to what had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. Its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masinissa">Masinissa</a>, a steadfast Roman ally in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>, died in 149 BCE and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a>. Micipsa's nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> had fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> and learned Roman manners and tactics there, and Micipsa adopted him and left the kingdom jointly to him and his own sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and Hiempsal. After his death Jugurtha had Hiempsal assassinated and defeated Adherbal, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. From 116 BCE a Roman commission led by Lucius Opimius divided Numidia, but Jugurtha bribed its members and received the better western half.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In 113 BCE Jugurtha again attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adherbal">Adherbal</a> and besieged him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>. A delegation under Marcus Scaurus failed to force him to a commitment, Adherbal surrendered, and Jugurtha had him executed along with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> who had joined the defence. The deaths of Roman citizens caused fury at home, and the Senate, threatened by the tribune Gaius Memmius, declared war with reluctance. The consul Lucius Calpurnius Bestia granted Jugurtha lenient terms, and Jugurtha bribed two tribunes to prevent his testimony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 110 BCE the consul Spurius Postumius Albinus achieved little, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-postumius-albinus">Aulus Postumius Albinus</a> was lured into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, lost half his army and agreed to a treaty, which the Senate refused to honour.</p><p>In 109 BCE Quintus Metellus, chosen for integrity, retrained the army and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-muthul">Battle of the Muthul</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a> escaped and turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare. Metellus took the fortress of Thala after forty days, but Jugurtha again escaped and took refuge with his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bocchus-i">Bocchus I</a> of Mauretania. The Tribal Assembly gave the command to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a>, elected consul in 107 BCE. Marius took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gafsa">Capsa</a> and fought the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> against both kings. Through negotiations <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a>, his quaestor, received Jugurtha in chains from Bocchus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bocchus annexed the western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jugurtha">Jugurtha</a>'s kingdom and was made a friend of the Roman people. Jugurtha was held in the Tullianum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and executed after appearing in Marius's triumph in 104 BCE. Numidia did not become a Roman province until 46 BCE. The war revealed the effect of corruption on Roman politics, and Marius's rise, with a command given by the Assembly against the Senate's usual practice, was followed by his later rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> the rivalry began over Sulla's role in the capture, which Sulla commemorated on a ring. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sallust">Sallust</a> wrote a monograph on the war, which with Plutarch's lives of Sulla and Marius is a main source.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jugurthine War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The historian Sallust, whose <b>Jugurthine War</b> is the main account of Numidia, was the first governor of the Roman province that replaced the kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
